A1. The last ground-up. ~ HAMBURGER
A9. Where a good fisherman puts his tackle in the winter. ~ ASIDE
A11. What fowl talk this one utters. ~ PARROT
A13. To put on the brakes. ~ RETARD
A15. This tells on Italians. ~ GARLIC
A17. Well! this settles it (slang). ~ UMP
A18. Its name is Mud. ~ MIRE
A20. When a Scotchman gives assent. ~ SELDOM
A22. This wise mother gets her children into woolen underwear early in the season. ~ MOTH
A24. The greatest water power on earth. ~ TEARS
A26. When you get this right, you will have a perfect fit. ~ SIZE
A27. The kind of case they are giving the college professors to handle. ~ BRIEF
A29. The pen that created "The Three Little Pigs." ~ STY
A30. She made a rattling good start when managed by an old crank. ~ LIZ
A31. These will take the wind out of your sails. ~ CALMS
A33. It will make the dumb dumber and the smart smarter. ~ ER
A35. A cry for silence. ~ OYEZ
A36. A bantam hen laid this egg. ~ EG
A38. You might get a way with this in Paris. ~ RUE
A39. Why! the little devil! ~ IMP
A41. A shining light in puzzledom. ~ RA
A42. A woman's word only. ~ LAST
A44. This belongs to N R A. ~ NR
A46. In the winter this beckons, "Come down and see me, s'metime." ~ SOUTH
A48. Make a happy landing. ~ LIT
A49. The old joints helped to make a crook out of this. ~ ARM
A51. This has more than one winter home in Florida. ~ MIAMI
A53. You may get it soon, and you may not. ~ ANON
A55. When this is on fire, it reminds one of Ed Wynn. ~ CHIEF
A57. Hoot Mon! tha' kin are a' here. ~ CLAN
A58. A body that never dries up. ~ SENATE
A60. To "git along" in a slow way. ~ PLOD
A62. In order to do this there must be competition. ~ VIE
A63. Amos says, "Dese are de places where de swallows downward fly". ~ DEBARS
A65. Here is a good scoop for some one. ~ TROWEL
A67. Wherefore art these? ~ ROMEOS
A69. You do not need a key to turn this lock, nature will do it for you. ~ TRESS
A70. This is always being turned down. ~ BEDSPREAD

D1. What's swat in baseball. ~ HIT
D2. A race starter. ~ ADAM
D3. We hope this will be worth more than gold in the new deal. ~ MERIT
D4. Same as above. ~ UP
D5. This made quite a clean-up in oil. ~ RAG
D6. A green that wears well. ~ GRASS
D7. Enraged Ragtime Ravers Establishments. ~ ERRE
D8. These are always crusty in the morning. ~ ROLLS
D9. A suit for damages. ~ ARMOR
D10. If you have this condition, tune in on a mouth wash. ~ SEPTIC
D12. When a man's desk is arranged this way, business is poor. ~ TIDILY
D14. This one may become a model in a Paris shop. ~ DRESS
D16. Less space and more cheer. ~ COZIER
D17. Farmers always wait for wet weather to raise these. ~ UMBRELLAS
D19. Feed. ~ EAT
D21. A low down story. ~ MEZZANINE
D23. Live Ticker. ~ HEART
D25. They were coming through this when they thought of "Sweet Adeline." ~ RYE
D28. He felt it in his bones when this new code was coming. ~ FLU
D32. The latest thing in shirts (plur.). ~ MEN
D34. A flow of Spanish. ~ RIO
D35. The kind of nerve it takes to look your creditors square in the face. ~ OPTIC
D37. Weighed and found mounting. ~ GAINED
D40. Hush! This is the word. ~ MUM
D43. This one should not live in a glass house. ~ STONER
D45. A college term that many classes repeat. ~ RAH
D46. What all fish are on Friday. ~ SMELT
D47. It took two of these to finish one quart of gin. ~ HALVES
D50. His wife did not know that he had this up his sleeve. ~ RIP
D52. These get heavier during the holiday season. ~ MAILS
D54. A doughboy. ~ NABOB
D55. Waxed. ~ CERED
D56. The strength of a piano. ~ FORTE
D59. What the "Big Bad Wolf" has become. ~ TAME
D61. She is so dumb that she thinks a tax striker is a hammer. ~ DORA
D64. You should get help for this one. ~ SOS
D66. The key for this lock should always be kept on a ring. ~ WED
D68. If you like spice, try this on ice. ~ SP


